\begin_layout Standard
The 
\family typewriter
offarray
\family default
 package lets you work with arrays (and matrices and vectors) whose indexing can start wherever you like—
 not just at 1 as in base-R,
 nor just at 0 as in misbegotten languages like C.
 This is great for applications like population dynamics:
 you can start your years at 2015,
 you can start your range of ages at 8,
 etc.
 Simpler,
 clearer,
 less buggity-bug-bug.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
New features in 
\family typewriter
offarray
\family default
 v2.1 are:
 
\family typewriter
autobayes
\family default
 (to reduce 3 steps to 1),
 and auto-clamping of array indices inside 
\family typewriter
autoloop
\family default
 via e.g.
 
\family typewriter
x[~i+j]
\family default
 to avoid OOB accesses (i.e.,
 in this case to limit 
\family typewriter
i+j
\family default
 to the range of allowed indices for the first dimension of 
\family typewriter
x
\family default
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Most R-ish things work as you would expect on 
\family typewriter
offarray
\family default
 objects.
 There is documentation!
 Try 
\family typewriter
?offarray
\family default
 (where exceptions are noted) and 
\family typewriter
utils::example('offarray','offarray')
\family default
.
\end_layout

