\begin_layout Section
Loops and speed
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When you need to to fill in an offarray one-element-at-a-time,
 for-loops are going to be slow,
 especially nested ones.
 A staggering amount of argument-checking gets done behind the scenes in R
\emph on
 every time
\emph default
 a single offarray element is read;
 and every time one element is changed,
 R makes a "deep copy"
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This deep-copy-on-subassignment seems to be unavoidable feature of R for any non-base-class object,
 certainly in S3 and AFAIK also in S4.
 Base-R arrays 
\emph on
can
\emph default
 do in-place modification,
 but there's no safe general way in your own code for your own classes,
 not even at the C level.
 I doubt R-core has much interest in changing this,
 although the word "might" has been bandied about for years.
 Packages like 
\family typewriter
data.table
\family default
 get round it only by defining new assignment operators,
 which I didn't want to do for humble 
\family typewriter
offarray
\family default
 objects.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of the entire offarray,
 which gets very costly for big offarrays.
 Even if you only intend to run your R code once for 
\family typewriter
RTMB::MakeADFun
\family default
,
 normal for-loops can 
\emph on
still
\emph default
 be waaaaay too slow.
 However,
 you can 
\emph on
completely solve the problem
\emph default
 by using 
\family typewriter
autoloop()
\family default
 to do (almost) all the loops for you,
 with 
\emph on
minimal
\emph default
 cost in comprehension.
 Cost in simple cases is about 2-3 times slower than base-R,
 ie negligible penalty.
 Here's an example:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
<<speed>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
N <- 20 # array has 8000 elements (smallish)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
system.time({
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
  xR <- array( 0,
 rep( N,
 3));
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
  for( i in 1:N) for( j in 1:N) for( k in 1:N) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
    xR[ i,j,k] <- 1/(i+2*j+3*k)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
})
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
system.time({
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
  xO <- offarray(0,
 dimseq=list( 1:N,
 1:N,
 1:N));
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
  for( i in 1:N) for( j in 1:N) for( k in 1:N) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
    xO[ i,j,k] <- 1/(i+2*j+3*k)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
})
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
system.time( 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
  xA <- autoloop( i=1:N,
 j=1:N,
 k=1:N,
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
    1/(i+2*j+3*k))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
N <- 100 # try 400 for a real test;
 then 8e6 elements and 512 MB
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
system.time( 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
  xA <- autoloop( i=1:N,
 j=1:N,
 k=1:N,
 1/(i+2*j+3*k))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
## Don't even TRY the direct loop over offarrays!!!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
system.time({
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
  xR <- array( 0,
 rep( N,
 3));
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
  for( i in 1:N) for( j in 1:N) for( k in 1:N) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
    xR[ i,j,k] <- 1/(i+2*j+3*k)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
})
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
