\begin_layout Section
Programming styles with 
\family typewriter
autoloop
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\family typewriter
autoloop
\family default
 auto-vectorizes your expression,
 and then evaluates it over subsets of a few thousand index-combinations at a time.
 Therefore,
 what it runs is not 
\emph on
quite
\emph default
 what you wrote,
 but if you stick to simple rules then you will never see the difference—
 and if you don't,
 it probably won't run at all.
 Basically,
 every statement within your 
\family typewriter
autoloop
\family default
 expression should result in a vector of the same length.
 Even though array-accesses don't 
\emph on
look
\emph default
 like they will do that,
 the magic of 
\family typewriter
autoloop
\family default
 ensures that they generally do
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Provided you stick to the rules.
 Are the rules documented?
 Not really,
 though there are some in 
\family typewriter
?autoloop
\family default
.
 Just stick to them anyway,
 is my advice.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mathematical operations within an 
\family typewriter
autoloop
\family default
 expression generally work fine (though not matrix-multiply—
 but that is simple to code manually within the expression).
 The main "limitation" is that you can't use 
\family typewriter
if
\family default
-statements
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Nor 
\family typewriter
for/while/repeat/break/next
\family default
,
 but 
\family typewriter
if
\family default
 is more significant here.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The R function 
\family typewriter
ifelse()
\family default
 can accomplish the same thing,
 but be aware that 
\family typewriter
ifelse(cond,ex1,ex2)
\family default
 always evaluates 
\emph on
both
\emph default
 
\family typewriter
ex1
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
ex2
\family default
 whether 
\family typewriter
cond
\family default
 is TRUE or FALSE.
 That can lead to OOB trouble if you naively try to access arrays,
 which is usually what I use 
\family typewriter
autoloop
\family default
 for.
 You can get around OOBs succinctly with 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

autoclamping
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 syntax such as 
\family typewriter
x[~y-b]
\family default
 (the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\family typewriter
~
\family default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 means that the index will be clamped to lie within the allowable range).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I'd like to put in a general Bayes-theorem example here,
 but I'd have to think about it,
 and who's got time for that?
 So instead,
 here's a no-thought example from Close-Kin Mark-Recapture.
 If you are currently or imminently doing the CKMR workshop,
 maybe defer reading this until Day 2!
 If not,
 you might not understand the background at all—
 never mind :)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a range of "adult" sampling years Y and ages-at-sampling A,
 and "juvenile" birth-years B,
 we consider a comparison between a potential parent "adult" and potential offspring "juvenile".
 The adult is lethally caught in year Ya at age Aa,
 and the juvenile in Yj at Aj.
 What is the demographic probability that the two will be a Parent-Offspring Pair,
 aka "POP"?
 In simple cases,
 that probability is "just" the adult's expected fecundity in the birth-year Bj (which will depend on the adult's age at Bj,
 plus whether the adult would actually be alive then),
 divided by the total fecundity across 
\emph on
all
\emph default
 adults in year B,
 which I've "pre-calculated" for simplicity.
 Let's make all this sex-specific while we're at it,
 because you always 
\emph on
should
\emph default
 in CKMR.
 And we'll use 
\family typewriter
autoloop
\family default
.
 Here's a naive example,
 showing off multi-statement code while we're again at it:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
<<ifelse-naive>>=
\end_layout

