\begin_layout Subsection
Using 
\family typewriter
autobayes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I often find myself using 
\family typewriter
autoloop
\family default
 for discrete Bayes calculations,
 e.g.
 to get 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\mathbb{P}\left[X|Y,Z\right]=\frac{\mathbb{P}\left[X,Y|Z\right]}{\mathbb{P}\left[Y|Z\right]}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

where the denominator is given by summing the numerator over all values of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 With pre-v2.0.74 
\family typewriter
offarray
\family default
,
 this requires 3 operations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
calculate an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unscaled
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 joint distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{P}\left[X,Y|Z\right]$
\end_inset

 via 
\family typewriter
autoloop()
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
use 
\family typewriter
sumover(...,'X')
\family default
 to get 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{P}\left[Y,Z\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
use 
\family typewriter
autoloop()
\family default
 again to divide the joint distribution by the denominator,
 for each value of 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This can now be done in one step just by calling 
\family typewriter
autobayes()
\family default
 instead of 
\family typewriter
autoloop()
\family default
 in step 1,
 specifying 
\family typewriter
RESPONSES=list( X=<xrange>)
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
CONDITIONERS=list(Y=<yrange>,Z=<zrange>)
\family default
.
 In fact,
 
\family typewriter
autobayes()
\family default
 is convenient whenever you want to normalize something to sum-to-1;
 see the Examples section of 
\family typewriter
?autoloop
\family default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
With package 
\family typewriter
RTMB
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This isn't the right place to document the 
\family typewriter
RTMB
\family default
 link!
 There should be a separate demo,
 probably in package 
\family typewriter
offartmb
\family default
.
 Anyway,
 the general point is:
 you can write a function using 
\family typewriter
offarray
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
autoloop
\family default
,
 and with minimal care you can evaluate it either directly in R,
 or via 
\family typewriter
RTMB::MakeADFun
\family default
,
 which creates a C-level version of your function 
\emph on
and 
\emph default
its derivative.
 That means it will run really fast,
 especially in the context of fitting to data.
 Plus,
 
\family typewriter
RTMB
\family default
 can automatically do Laplace approximation to integrate out random effects,
 etc etc.
 WOW!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What you need to do:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Load the package 
\family typewriter
offartmb
\family default
,
 after
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
I have added a hack that tries to make the loading-order irrelevant.
 "Tries".
\end_layout

\end_inset

 loading 
\family typewriter
offarray
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
RTMB
\family default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Wrap the body of your "objective function" in a call to 
\family typewriter
reclasso
\family default
,
 as shown below.
 This will have 
\emph on
no effect
\emph default
 if you are running the function directly in R,
 but will also make it work nicely with 
\family typewriter
RTMB::MakeADFun
\family default
.
 It won't work if you don't.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If you want to store variables calculated inside your objective function,
 so they are available afterwards,
 name them in a call to 
\family typewriter
REPORTO()
\family default
 somewhere (like the C macro 
\family typewriter
REPORT()
\family default
 in good old TMB).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Avoid comparison-operations in your code,
 eg "==",
 ">".
 RTMB hates them.
 You might be able to get away with them if they only apply to "data",
 but in that case you are better off doing them beforehand and storing the results as additional data objects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Good practice is to put the data needed by your function into a separate 
\family typewriter
environment
\family default
,
 and attach that environment to your function.
 The things from 
\family typewriter
REPORTO()
\family default
 will be stored there too.
 This is a general principle well worth learning about in R—
 not specific to 
\family typewriter
RTMB
\family default
,
 but perhaps particularly relevant there.
 The CKMR course examples all do it.
\end_layout

